window.__SHB_EGYPTIAN_BOOK_QUESTIONS__=(window.__SHB_EGYPTIAN_BOOK_QUESTIONS__||[]).concat([{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q001","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Thelowerunitofthenormalhaemoglobinatbirthis","o":["12g%","14g%","16g%","18g%"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The course defines neonatal anaemia as haemoglobin below 14 g/dL and notes that normal haemoglobin is above 14 g/dL at birth. Thus 14 g/dL is the lower limit among the supplied choices. Interpretation still depends on gestational and postnatal age.","more":"The course defines neonatal anaemia as haemoglobin below 14 g/dL and notes that normal haemoglobin is above 14 g/dL at birth. Thus 14 g/dL is the lower limit among the supplied choices. Interpretation still depends on gestational and postnatal age.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.3 and 5: neonatal haemoglobin reference and definition of anaemia.\n\nHigh-yield point: The course defines neonatal anaemia as haemoglobin below 14 g/dL and notes that normal haemoglobin is above 14 g/dL at birth.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q001 — Q1: Lower limit of normal haemoglobin at birth","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 94 · Printed page 175 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q1","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q004","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Thefollowingarethenormalhaemoglobinpresentin postnatallife,except","o":["HaemoglobinA1","HaemoglobinA2","HaemoglobinF","HaemoglobinF2"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"The major normal haemoglobin fractions are HbA (α2β2), HbA2 (α2δ2) and HbF (α2γ2). “HbF2” is not a standard normal haemoglobin fraction. The source answer d is consistent with the course.","more":"The major normal haemoglobin fractions are HbA (α2β2), HbA2 (α2δ2) and HbF (α2γ2). “HbF2” is not a standard normal haemoglobin fraction. The source answer d is consistent with the course.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.3: normal haemoglobin chain compositions and proportions.\n\nHigh-yield point: The major normal haemoglobin fractions are HbA (α2β2), HbA2 (α2δ2) and HbF (α2γ2).","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q004 — Q4: Normal haemoglobin types in postnatal life: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 94 · Printed page 175 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q4","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q005","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Theaminoacidmakeupoftheadulthaemoglobin","o":["α2β2","α1β1","α2γ2","α1γ1"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Adult HbA contains two alpha and two beta globin chains (α2β2). HbA2 is α2δ2 and HbF is α2γ2.","more":"Adult HbA contains two alpha and two beta globin chains (α2β2). HbA2 is α2δ2 and HbF is α2γ2.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.3: haemoglobin structure.\n\nHigh-yield point: Adult HbA contains two alpha and two beta globin chains (α2β2).","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q005 — Q5: Globin-chain composition of adult haemoglobin","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 94 · Printed page 175 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q5","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q007","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"The following are the causes of normocytic normochromicanaemia,except","o":["Renalfailure","Liverdisease","BJ2deficiency","Infection"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Normocytic normochromic anaemia may occur with renal failure, chronic infection/inflammation, acute blood loss and some liver/systemic disorders. Vitamin B12 deficiency classically causes megaloblastic macrocytic anaemia, so it is the intended exception.","more":"Normocytic normochromic anaemia may occur with renal failure, chronic infection/inflammation, acute blood loss and some liver/systemic disorders. Vitamin B12 deficiency classically causes megaloblastic macrocytic anaemia, so it is the intended exception.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.4–5: red-cell indices and causes/classification of anaemia.\n\nHigh-yield point: Normocytic normochromic anaemia may occur with renal failure, chronic infection/inflammation, acute blood loss and some liver/systemic disorders.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q007 — Q7: Causes of normocytic normochromic anaemia: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 95 · Printed page 176 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q7","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q011","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Themostcommoncausesofdeficiencyanaemia","o":["Folicacid","G6PD","Iron","B12"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Iron deficiency is the most common nutritional deficiency causing anaemia in children. It is commonly due to inadequate dietary intake, increased requirements during growth or blood loss.","more":"Iron deficiency is the most common nutritional deficiency causing anaemia in children. It is commonly due to inadequate dietary intake, increased requirements during growth or blood loss.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.6 onward: iron-deficiency anaemia is identified as the most common paediatric anaemia.\n\nHigh-yield point: Iron deficiency is the most common nutritional deficiency causing anaemia in children.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q011 — Q11: Most common nutritional deficiency anaemia","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 95 · Printed page 176 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q11","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q012","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Theabsorptionoftheironisenhancedin","o":["Irondeficiencyanaemia","Haemorrhage","Both","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Iron absorption increases when body iron stores are depleted and erythropoietic demand rises. Iron deficiency suppresses hepcidin, increasing intestinal iron uptake; blood loss also depletes iron and can stimulate absorption during recovery.","more":"Iron absorption increases when body iron stores are depleted and erythropoietic demand rises. Iron deficiency suppresses hepcidin, increasing intestinal iron uptake; blood loss also depletes iron and can stimulate absorption during recovery.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.6–9: iron-deficiency pathophysiology, assessment and treatment.\n\nHigh-yield point: Iron absorption increases when body iron stores are depleted and erythropoietic demand rises.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q012 — Q12: Factors that enhance intestinal iron absorption","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 95 · Printed page 176 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q12","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q013","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Thesiteoftheabsorptionoftheoraliron","o":["Duodenum","Jejunum","Stomach","Smallintestine"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Non-haem iron is absorbed predominantly in the duodenum and proximal jejunum. The duodenum is the best answer among these options.","more":"Non-haem iron is absorbed predominantly in the duodenum and proximal jejunum. The duodenum is the best answer among these options.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.6–8: iron absorption and iron-deficiency anaemia.\n\nHigh-yield point: Non-haem iron is absorbed predominantly in the duodenum and proximal jejunum.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q013 — Q13: Main site of oral iron absorption","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 95 · Printed page 176 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q13","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q015","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Thebiochemicalparameterfordiagnosisofiron deficiencyanaemia","o":["Totalironbindingcapacity","Iron","Ferritin","Haemosiderin"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Serum ferritin reflects iron stores and is typically low in iron deficiency; it is the most useful single biochemical marker in an otherwise uncomplicated case. Ferritin is also an acute-phase reactant and may be normal or raised during inflammation, so interpret it with the clinical context and other iron studies.","more":"Serum ferritin reflects iron stores and is typically low in iron deficiency; it is the most useful single biochemical marker in an otherwise uncomplicated case. Ferritin is also an acute-phase reactant and may be normal or raised during inflammation, so interpret it with the clinical context and other iron studies.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.8–10: iron studies, serum ferritin, serum iron and TIBC.\n\nHigh-yield point: Serum ferritin reflects iron stores and is typically low in iron deficiency; it is the most useful single biochemical marker in an otherwise uncomplicated case.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q015 — Q15: Biochemical parameter used to diagnose iron deficiency anaemia","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 95 · Printed page 176 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q15","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q016","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Thefirstlaboratoryevidenceasaresponsetoiron treatment","o":["IncreasedRBCs","Increasedhaemoglobin","Reticulocytosis","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"A reticulocyte response occurs before a measurable rise in haemoglobin after effective iron replacement. The reticulocyte count typically increases within about a week, with haemoglobin rising later over subsequent weeks.","more":"A reticulocyte response occurs before a measurable rise in haemoglobin after effective iron replacement. The reticulocyte count typically increases within about a week, with haemoglobin rising later over subsequent weeks.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.4: reticulocyte count reflects bone-marrow activity and is an early response marker in iron therapy.\n\nHigh-yield point: A reticulocyte response occurs before a measurable rise in haemoglobin after effective iron replacement.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q016 — Q16: First laboratory evidence of response to iron treatment","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 96 · Printed page 177 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q16","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q018","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Themostidealsaltofiron","o":["Ferroussulphate","Ferricsulphate","Both","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Ferrous salts are generally better absorbed than ferric salts. Ferrous sulphate is a standard, widely used oral preparation for iron deficiency.","more":"Ferrous salts are generally better absorbed than ferric salts. Ferrous sulphate is a standard, widely used oral preparation for iron deficiency.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.8–9: oral iron therapy and preparations.\n\nHigh-yield point: Ferrous salts are generally better absorbed than ferric salts.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q018 — Q18: Preferred oral iron salt","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 96 · Printed page 177 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q18","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q019","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Treatment","q":"Themostidealrouteofadministrationofiron","o":["Oral","Intramuscular","Intravenous","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Oral iron is preferred for most children with uncomplicated iron deficiency anaemia. Parenteral iron is reserved for selected circumstances such as malabsorption, intolerance, poor response despite adherence or a need for rapid repletion in selected patients.","more":"Oral iron is preferred for most children with uncomplicated iron deficiency anaemia. Parenteral iron is reserved for selected circumstances such as malabsorption, intolerance, poor response despite adherence or a need for rapid repletion in selected patients.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.9: oral and parenteral iron indications.\n\nHigh-yield point: Oral iron is preferred for most children with uncomplicated iron deficiency anaemia.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q019 — Q19: Preferred route of iron administration","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 96 · Printed page 177 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q19","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q020","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Ironshouldnotbegivenaftermealsbecauseof","o":["Decreasedabsorption","Increasedabsorption","Both","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Food can reduce non-haem iron absorption, particularly when it contains calcium, phytates, tannins or other inhibitors. Iron is generally absorbed better on an empty stomach, although taking it with a small amount of food may improve tolerability when gastrointestinal adverse effects limit adherence.","more":"Food can reduce non-haem iron absorption, particularly when it contains calcium, phytates, tannins or other inhibitors. Iron is generally absorbed better on an empty stomach, although taking it with a small amount of food may improve tolerability when gastrointestinal adverse effects limit adherence.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.8–9: oral iron administration and factors affecting absorption.\n\nHigh-yield point: Food can reduce non-haem iron absorption, particularly when it contains calcium, phytates, tannins or other inhibitors.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q020 — Q20: Why iron is not ideally taken with meals","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 96 · Printed page 177 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q20","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q021","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Nutrition","q":"Thebreastmilkcanbeasourceofironfor","o":["2months","3months","9months","6months"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Breast milk contains relatively little iron, but its bioavailability is high. Healthy term infants generally have prenatal iron stores that support early infancy; around 4–6 months, complementary iron-containing foods or supplementation according to local guidance become important. The course/question-bank convention uses 6 months as the answer. This is a simplified population-level teaching point, not a guarantee that every infant’s iron needs are met until exactly 6 months.","more":"Breast milk contains relatively little iron, but its bioavailability is high. Healthy term infants generally have prenatal iron stores that support early infancy; around 4–6 months, complementary iron-containing foods or supplementation according to local guidance become important. The course/question-bank convention uses 6 months as the answer. This is a simplified population-level teaching point, not a guarantee that every infant’s iron needs are met until exactly 6 months.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth and Nutrition 2024, pp.30–32: breastfeeding and nutrient composition; Hematology 2024, pp.6–9: iron requirement/deficiency. Extended because it combines infant nutrition and hematology and the duration is a simplified rule.\n\nHigh-yield point: Breast milk contains relatively little iron, but its bioavailability is high.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q021 — Q21: Duration for which breast milk provides iron","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 96 · Printed page 177 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q21","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q028","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"ThesiteofabsorptionoftheB12","o":["Duodenum","Jejunum","Terminalileum","Caecum"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Vitamin B12 binds intrinsic factor in the stomach/duodenum and the complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum.","more":"Vitamin B12 binds intrinsic factor in the stomach/duodenum and the complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.5–6: megaloblastic anaemia and B12 deficiency; standard GI absorption physiology.\n\nHigh-yield point: Vitamin B12 binds intrinsic factor in the stomach/duodenum and the complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q028 — Q28: Site of vitamin B12 absorption","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 97 · Printed page 178 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q28","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q030","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Thenutrientdeficiencyproducingthehaemolytic anaemia","o":["VitaminE","VitaminB12","Iron","Folicacid"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Vitamin E deficiency can cause oxidative injury to red-cell membranes and haemolysis, particularly in premature infants or infants with fat malabsorption. Iron deficiency causes microcytic anaemia rather than haemolysis, while B12/folate deficiencies cause ineffective erythropoiesis/megaloblastic anaemia.","more":"Vitamin E deficiency can cause oxidative injury to red-cell membranes and haemolysis, particularly in premature infants or infants with fat malabsorption. Iron deficiency causes microcytic anaemia rather than haemolysis, while B12/folate deficiencies cause ineffective erythropoiesis/megaloblastic anaemia.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.5–6: classification of anaemia and nutritional deficiency; neonatal context may be covered in Neonatology.\n\nHigh-yield point: Vitamin E deficiency can cause oxidative injury to red-cell membranes and haemolysis, particularly in premature infants or infants with fat malabsorption.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q030 — Q30: Nutrient deficiency causing haemolytic anaemia","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 97 · Printed page 178 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q30","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q031","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Thesignificanceofthehaptoglobin","o":["Increasedinhaemolysis","Reducedinhaemolysis","Both","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Haptoglobin binds free plasma haemoglobin. During intravascular haemolysis, circulating free haemoglobin consumes haptoglobin, so the serum concentration falls. It should be interpreted with LDH, indirect bilirubin, reticulocytes and the blood film.","more":"Haptoglobin binds free plasma haemoglobin. During intravascular haemolysis, circulating free haemoglobin consumes haptoglobin, so the serum concentration falls. It should be interpreted with LDH, indirect bilirubin, reticulocytes and the blood film.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.4: haemolysis indices include high indirect bilirubin/LDH and low haptoglobin.\n\nHigh-yield point: Haptoglobin binds free plasma haemoglobin.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q031 — Q31: Significance of haptoglobin in haemolysis","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 97 · Printed page 178 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q31","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q033","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"ThemostsignificantabnormalityHbelectrophoresis inthalasemicminors","o":["IncreasedHbA2","IncreasedHbA1","DecreasedHbA2","DecreasedHbA1"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Beta-thalassaemia trait typically shows increased HbA2 on haemoglobin electrophoresis, often with a mild increase in HbF in some cases. HbA1 is not the relevant electrophoretic fraction in this comparison.","more":"Beta-thalassaemia trait typically shows increased HbA2 on haemoglobin electrophoresis, often with a mild increase in HbF in some cases. HbA1 is not the relevant electrophoretic fraction in this comparison.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.17–18: beta-thalassaemia trait and haemoglobin electrophoresis.\n\nHigh-yield point: Beta-thalassaemia trait typically shows increased HbA2 on haemoglobin electrophoresis, often with a mild increase in HbF in some cases.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q033 — Q33: Most significant electrophoresis abnormality in beta-thalassaemia minor","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 98 · Printed page 179 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q33","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q035","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The factors promoting sickling are as follows, except","o":["Anoxia","Acidosis","Infection","Overhydration"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Deoxygenation, acidosis, infection/inflammation and dehydration promote HbS polymerization and sickling. Overhydration is not a recognized sickling trigger; dehydration and increased blood viscosity are the relevant risks.","more":"Deoxygenation, acidosis, infection/inflammation and dehydration promote HbS polymerization and sickling. Overhydration is not a recognized sickling trigger; dehydration and increased blood viscosity are the relevant risks.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.21–24: sickle-cell disease pathophysiology and crisis precipitants.\n\nHigh-yield point: Deoxygenation, acidosis, infection/inflammation and dehydration promote HbS polymerization and sickling.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q035 — Q35: Factors promoting sickling: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 98 · Printed page 179 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q35","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q037","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Complications","q":"Sicklecelldiseasepatientissusceptibletothe followinginfection","o":["Pneumococcal","Salmonella","Both","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Functional asplenia increases susceptibility to encapsulated organisms, especially Streptococcus pneumoniae. Salmonella species are classically associated with osteomyelitis in sickle-cell disease. Both organisms are clinically relevant.","more":"Functional asplenia increases susceptibility to encapsulated organisms, especially Streptococcus pneumoniae. Salmonella species are classically associated with osteomyelitis in sickle-cell disease. Both organisms are clinically relevant.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.21–24: sickle-cell disease, infection risk, osteomyelitis and preventive care.\n\nHigh-yield point: Functional asplenia increases susceptibility to encapsulated organisms, especially Streptococcus pneumoniae.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q037 — Q37: Infections to which patients with sickle-cell disease are susceptible","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 98 · Printed page 179 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q37","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q038","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Thechemicalusedtopromotesicklingamongblood smear","o":["2%sodiummetabisulphate","10%sodiummetabisulphate","2%sodiumsulphate","2%sodiumchloride"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Sodium metabisulphite is a reducing agent that lowers oxygen tension and induces sickling in a fresh blood preparation. A 2% solution is the traditional laboratory teaching answer. Screening by sickling preparation does not replace confirmatory haemoglobin analysis/electrophoresis.","more":"Sodium metabisulphite is a reducing agent that lowers oxygen tension and induces sickling in a fresh blood preparation. A 2% solution is the traditional laboratory teaching answer. Screening by sickling preparation does not replace confirmatory haemoglobin analysis/electrophoresis.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.21–23: sickle-cell disease diagnosis and laboratory investigations.\n\nHigh-yield point: Sodium metabisulphite is a reducing agent that lowers oxygen tension and induces sickling in a fresh blood preparation.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q038 — Q38: Chemical used to induce sickling on a blood smear","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 98 · Printed page 179 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q38","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q039","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Thespecifictesttoconfirmcongenital spherocytosis","o":["Reticulocytecount","RBCscount","Osmoticfragilitytest","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Osmotic fragility testing is a traditional test for hereditary spherocytosis: spherocytes lyse at higher saline concentrations because of reduced membrane surface-area-to-volume ratio. Contemporary diagnostic approaches commonly include eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) binding by flow cytometry, with family history, blood film, reticulocytosis and hemolysis markers. The course itself identifies EMA as a newer test, so the question is retained as a historically taught test but marked as extended/older practice.","more":"Osmotic fragility testing is a traditional test for hereditary spherocytosis: spherocytes lyse at higher saline concentrations because of reduced membrane surface-area-to-volume ratio. Contemporary diagnostic approaches commonly include eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) binding by flow cytometry, with family history, blood film, reticulocytosis and hemolysis markers. The course itself identifies EMA as a newer test, so the question is retained as a historically taught test but marked as extended/older practice.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.16: hereditary spherocytosis; lecture mentions EMA test as a newer investigation. Extended Beyond Core Curriculum because the question emphasizes the older osmotic-fragility method.\n\nHigh-yield point: Osmotic fragility testing is a traditional test for hereditary spherocytosis: spherocytes lyse at higher saline concentrations because of reduced membrane surface-area-to-volume ratio.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q039 — Q39: Specific test for hereditary spherocytosis","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 99 · Printed page 180 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q39","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q044","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Bleedingstopsspontaneouslybecause,except","o":["Vascularspasm","Plateletplugging","Clotting","RBCsadhesion"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Primary haemostasis begins with vascular spasm and platelet adhesion/activation/aggregation to form a platelet plug. Secondary haemostasis generates fibrin to stabilize the plug. Red-cell adhesion is not a standard haemostatic mechanism.","more":"Primary haemostasis begins with vascular spasm and platelet adhesion/activation/aggregation to form a platelet plug. Secondary haemostasis generates fibrin to stabilize the plug. Red-cell adhesion is not a standard haemostatic mechanism.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.30–33: coagulation cascade and evaluation of bleeding disorders.\n\nHigh-yield point: Primary haemostasis begins with vascular spasm and platelet adhesion/activation/aggregation to form a platelet plug.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q044 — Q44: Mechanisms responsible for spontaneous cessation of bleeding: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 99 · Printed page 180 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q44","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q046","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"The causes of non-thrombocytopenic purpura except","o":["Vascular","Thrombasthenia","Haenoch-Scholeinpurpural","Idiopathicthrombocytopenicpurpura"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Purpura with a normal platelet count can result from vascular inflammation (for example IgA vasculitis/Henoch–Schönlein purpura) or platelet function disorders such as Glanzmann thrombasthenia. Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) is defined by low platelet count and therefore is the exception.","more":"Purpura with a normal platelet count can result from vascular inflammation (for example IgA vasculitis/Henoch–Schönlein purpura) or platelet function disorders such as Glanzmann thrombasthenia. Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) is defined by low platelet count and therefore is the exception.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.30–34 and thrombocytopenia section: platelet-number/function and vascular causes of bleeding/purpura.\n\nHigh-yield point: Purpura with a normal platelet count can result from vascular inflammation (for example IgA vasculitis/Henoch–Schönlein purpura) or platelet function disorders such as Glanzmann thrombasthenia.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q046 — Q46: Causes of non-thrombocytopenic purpura: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 99 · Printed page 180 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q46","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q051","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Treatment","q":"The blood components may be used forthe replacementtreatmentinhaemophilia,except","o":["FactorFVIII","FactorFIX","Both","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Haemophilia A is due to factor VIII deficiency and haemophilia B to factor IX deficiency. Replacement therapy uses the deficient factor (or suitable bypassing/non-factor therapies in selected cases). Thus factors VIII and IX are both used, but in different haemophilia types; there is no single exception among them.","more":"Haemophilia A is due to factor VIII deficiency and haemophilia B to factor IX deficiency. Replacement therapy uses the deficient factor (or suitable bypassing/non-factor therapies in selected cases). Thus factors VIII and IX are both used, but in different haemophilia types; there is no single exception among them.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.30–33 and hemophilia section: coagulation factor deficiency and factor replacement.\n\nHigh-yield point: Haemophilia A is due to factor VIII deficiency and haemophilia B to factor IX deficiency.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q051 — Q51: Blood components/factors used for replacement in haemophilia: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 100 · Printed page 181 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q51","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q053","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"The causes of acquired coagulation factor deficiency,except","o":["VitaminKdeficiency","DIC","Antinuclearantibodies","Haemophilia"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Acquired factor deficiency can occur with vitamin K deficiency, liver disease, consumption in DIC or acquired inhibitors/autoantibodies against clotting factors. Haemophilia A/B are inherited factor deficiencies, so haemophilia is the exception.","more":"Acquired factor deficiency can occur with vitamin K deficiency, liver disease, consumption in DIC or acquired inhibitors/autoantibodies against clotting factors. Haemophilia A/B are inherited factor deficiencies, so haemophilia is the exception.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.30–34: coagulation cascade, acquired and inherited bleeding disorders.\n\nHigh-yield point: Acquired factor deficiency can occur with vitamin K deficiency, liver disease, consumption in DIC or acquired inhibitors/autoantibodies against clotting factors.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q053 — Q53: Acquired coagulation-factor deficiency: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 100 · Printed page 181 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q53","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q054","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"The test used to diagnose fibrinolysis in disseminatedintravascularcoagulopathy(DIC)","o":["Bleedingtime","Clottingtime","Fibrinogendegradationproduct","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"DIC causes systemic activation of coagulation with consumption of platelets and clotting factors, together with secondary fibrinolysis. Fibrin degradation products (FDPs) and D-dimer are increased; fibrinogen is often reduced and PT/aPTT may be prolonged.","more":"DIC causes systemic activation of coagulation with consumption of platelets and clotting factors, together with secondary fibrinolysis. Fibrin degradation products (FDPs) and D-dimer are increased; fibrinogen is often reduced and PT/aPTT may be prolonged.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.30–34: coagulation cascade and DIC/coagulation investigations.\n\nHigh-yield point: DIC causes systemic activation of coagulation with consumption of platelets and clotting factors, together with secondary fibrinolysis.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q054 — Q54: Laboratory test used to detect fibrinolysis in DIC","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 100 · Printed page 181 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q54","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q055","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Conditioninwhichprothromintimeisprolongedbut partialthromboplastintimeisnormal","o":["FactorVIIdeficiency","FactorIIdeficiency","Both","None"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Factor VII belongs to the extrinsic pathway, so isolated factor VII deficiency typically prolongs prothrombin time while leaving activated partial thromboplastin time normal. Factor II is in the common pathway and can prolong both tests.","more":"Factor VII belongs to the extrinsic pathway, so isolated factor VII deficiency typically prolongs prothrombin time while leaving activated partial thromboplastin time normal. Factor II is in the common pathway and can prolong both tests.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.30–33: coagulation cascade, extrinsic/intrinsic/common pathways and screening tests.\n\nHigh-yield point: Factor VII belongs to the extrinsic pathway, so isolated factor VII deficiency typically prolongs prothrombin time while leaving activated partial thromboplastin time normal.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q055 — Q55: Prolonged PT with normal PTT","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 100 · Printed page 181 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q55","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q056","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Partial thromboplastin time is prolonged in deficiencyoffactor,except","o":["VII","IX","XI","V"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"aPTT evaluates the intrinsic and common pathways. Deficiencies of factors IX, XI and V can prolong aPTT; factor VII is part of the extrinsic pathway and prolongs PT instead.","more":"aPTT evaluates the intrinsic and common pathways. Deficiencies of factors IX, XI and V can prolong aPTT; factor VII is part of the extrinsic pathway and prolongs PT instead.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.30–33: coagulation cascade and interpretation of PT/aPTT.\n\nHigh-yield point: aPTT evaluates the intrinsic and common pathways.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q056 — Q56: Factor deficiency that does not prolong aPTT: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 101 · Printed page 182 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q56","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q057","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Conditioninwhichbothprothromintimeandpartial thromboplastintimeareprolongedexcept","o":["FactorVdeficiency","FactorXdeficiency","Liverdisease","FactorXIdeficiency"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"PT and aPTT can both be prolonged when common-pathway factors (II, V, X, fibrinogen) are deficient, or in severe liver disease/DIC. Factor XI is an intrinsic-pathway factor and typically prolongs aPTT with PT remaining normal.","more":"PT and aPTT can both be prolonged when common-pathway factors (II, V, X, fibrinogen) are deficient, or in severe liver disease/DIC. Factor XI is an intrinsic-pathway factor and typically prolongs aPTT with PT remaining normal.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.30–33: coagulation cascade and screening tests.\n\nHigh-yield point: PT and aPTT can both be prolonged when common-pathway factors (II, V, X, fibrinogen) are deficient, or in severe liver disease/DIC.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q057 — Q57: Prolongation of both PT and aPTT: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 101 · Printed page 182 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q57","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q061","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Thefollowingisnotcharacteristicofthalassemia major","o":["Hypochromicmicrocyticanaemia","Splenomegaly","Targetcells","Decreasedserumiron"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Beta-thalassaemia major causes severe microcytic hypochromic anaemia, ineffective erythropoiesis, haemolysis, splenomegaly and target cells. Chronic transfusion and increased intestinal absorption can cause iron overload, so serum iron is not typically decreased in established transfusion-dependent disease.","more":"Beta-thalassaemia major causes severe microcytic hypochromic anaemia, ineffective erythropoiesis, haemolysis, splenomegaly and target cells. Chronic transfusion and increased intestinal absorption can cause iron overload, so serum iron is not typically decreased in established transfusion-dependent disease.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.17–20: beta-thalassaemia major, chronic haemolysis, transfusion dependence and iron overload.\n\nHigh-yield point: Beta-thalassaemia major causes severe microcytic hypochromic anaemia, ineffective erythropoiesis, haemolysis, splenomegaly and target cells.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q061 — Q61: Finding not characteristic of thalassaemia major","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 101 · Printed page 182 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q61","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q062","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Thecommonestcauseoftheaplasticanaemia","o":["Idiopathic","Chloramphinicol","Phenylbutazone","Petroleumproducts"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"In acquired aplastic anaemia, many cases are classified as idiopathic because no specific cause is identified after evaluation. Drugs, toxins, viral infections and immune-mediated mechanisms are recognized causes, but idiopathic/immune-mediated disease is the commonest broad category in standard teaching.","more":"In acquired aplastic anaemia, many cases are classified as idiopathic because no specific cause is identified after evaluation. Drugs, toxins, viral infections and immune-mediated mechanisms are recognized causes, but idiopathic/immune-mediated disease is the commonest broad category in standard teaching.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.27–29: aplastic anaemia, acquired/congenital causes and investigations.\n\nHigh-yield point: In acquired aplastic anaemia, many cases are classified as idiopathic because no specific cause is identified after evaluation.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q062 — Q62: Most common cause of aplastic anaemia","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 102 · Printed page 183 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q62","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q064","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Treatment","q":"Ideally, children with thalassemia should be transfusedwith","o":["PackedRBC","Plateletrichplasma","WashedRBC","Bloodofthepolycythemic"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Children with transfusion-dependent thalassaemia should receive packed red blood cells (leukoreduced and appropriately matched according to transfusion protocol), not plasma-rich components. This provides red-cell mass while limiting unnecessary plasma exposure.","more":"Children with transfusion-dependent thalassaemia should receive packed red blood cells (leukoreduced and appropriately matched according to transfusion protocol), not plasma-rich components. This provides red-cell mass while limiting unnecessary plasma exposure.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.19–20: transfusion-dependent beta-thalassaemia and transfusion complications.\n\nHigh-yield point: Children with transfusion-dependent thalassaemia should receive packed red blood cells (leukoreduced and appropriately matched according to transfusion protocol), not plasma-rich components.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q064 — Q64: Preferred blood component for transfusion in thalassaemia","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 102 · Printed page 183 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q64","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q065","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Purpuraiscommonfeatureof","o":["Alphathalassemia","Neimann-Pickdisease","Miliarytuberculosis","Congenitalrubella"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Congenital rubella can cause thrombocytopenia and a blueberry-muffin rash, producing purpuric or violaceous lesions. It is the classic answer among these options.","more":"Congenital rubella can cause thrombocytopenia and a blueberry-muffin rash, producing purpuric or violaceous lesions. It is the classic answer among these options.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, congenital/infection-related purpura; Neonatology 2024–25, congenital infection manifestations.\n\nHigh-yield point: Congenital rubella can cause thrombocytopenia and a blueberry-muffin rash, producing purpuric or violaceous lesions.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q065 — Q65: Congenital disease commonly associated with purpura","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 102 · Printed page 183 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q65","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q068","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Themajorsiteofacutehaemopoesisinafoetusof 5monthis","o":["Spleen","Liver","Lymphmodes","Bonemarrow"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Haemopoiesis begins in the yolk sac early in embryogenesis, then shifts predominantly to the liver and spleen during fetal life; bone marrow becomes the principal site later in gestation and throughout postnatal life. At five months, the liver is the main site.","more":"Haemopoiesis begins in the yolk sac early in embryogenesis, then shifts predominantly to the liver and spleen during fetal life; bone marrow becomes the principal site later in gestation and throughout postnatal life. At five months, the liver is the main site.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.3: yolk sac in first weeks, liver/spleen from around 8 weeks to 6 months, bone marrow postnatally.\n\nHigh-yield point: Haemopoiesis begins in the yolk sac early in embryogenesis, then shifts predominantly to the liver and spleen during fetal life; bone marrow becomes the principal site later in gestation and throughout postnatal life.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q068 — Q68: Main site of fetal haemopoiesis at five months","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 102 · Printed page 183 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q68","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q072","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Commonesthaematologicalmalignancyinchildren is","o":["Chroniclymphocyticleukaemia","Chronicmyeloidleukaemia","Acutemyeloidleukaemia","Acutelymphocyticleukaemia"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) is the most common childhood haematological malignancy and the most common childhood leukaemia.","more":"Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) is the most common childhood haematological malignancy and the most common childhood leukaemia.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.2: leukemia/lymphoma listed; standard pediatric hematology epidemiology.\n\nHigh-yield point: Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) is the most common childhood haematological malignancy and the most common childhood leukaemia.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q072 — Q72: Commonest haematological malignancy in children","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 103 · Printed page 184 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q72","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q073","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"Autohaemolysistestinpositivein","o":["Beta-thalassemia","Hereditaryspherocytosis","VitaminEdeficiency","Sicklecelldisease"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The classic autohaemolysis test is increased in hereditary spherocytosis and may improve with added glucose. It is an older diagnostic method; contemporary workup uses EMA binding, osmotic fragility and clinical/laboratory findings.","more":"The classic autohaemolysis test is increased in hereditary spherocytosis and may improve with added glucose. It is an older diagnostic method; contemporary workup uses EMA binding, osmotic fragility and clinical/laboratory findings.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.16: hereditary spherocytosis and diagnostic testing (including newer EMA assay).\n\nHigh-yield point: The classic autohaemolysis test is increased in hereditary spherocytosis and may improve with added glucose.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q073 — Q73: Positive autohaemolysis test","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 103 · Printed page 184 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q73","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q076","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Macrocytosisisseeninallofthefollowing,except","o":["Hypothyroidism","Folicaciddeficiency","Thalassemiamajor","B12deficiency"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Macrocytosis is seen with B12/folate deficiency, hypothyroidism, liver disease, reticulocytosis and some medications. Thalassaemia causes microcytosis and hypochromia, so it is the exception.","more":"Macrocytosis is seen with B12/folate deficiency, hypothyroidism, liver disease, reticulocytosis and some medications. Thalassaemia causes microcytosis and hypochromia, so it is the exception.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.4–5 and pp.17–18: MCV classification and thalassaemia.\n\nHigh-yield point: Macrocytosis is seen with B12/folate deficiency, hypothyroidism, liver disease, reticulocytosis and some medications.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q076 — Q76: Macrocytosis: exception","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 103 · Printed page 184 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q76","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q078","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Trueregardingfetalhaemoglobin","o":["HashigherFecontent","OnlyHbinintrauterinelife","PredominantHbinlatermonthofinfant","Resistanttoalkalidenaturation"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"HbF (α2γ2) has a higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA and is resistant to alkali denaturation compared with adult haemoglobin. It is the predominant haemoglobin in fetal life but does not remain the only haemoglobin throughout infancy.","more":"HbF (α2γ2) has a higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA and is resistant to alkali denaturation compared with adult haemoglobin. It is the predominant haemoglobin in fetal life but does not remain the only haemoglobin throughout infancy.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, p.3: HbF structure and normal proportions at birth/adulthood.\n\nHigh-yield point: HbF (α2γ2) has a higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA and is resistant to alkali denaturation compared with adult haemoglobin.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q078 — Q78: True statement about fetal haemoglobin","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 104 · Printed page 185 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q78","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-haematology-accepted-CH07-Q081","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Thecommonestcauseoftheanaemiainchildhood","o":["VitB12deficiency","Pyridoxinedeficiency","Folicaciddeficiency","Irondeficiency"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Iron deficiency is the commonest cause of anaemia in children worldwide, particularly during periods of rapid growth and when dietary iron is inadequate.","more":"Iron deficiency is the commonest cause of anaemia in children worldwide, particularly during periods of rapid growth and when dietary iron is inadequate.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Hematology 2024, pp.5–9: iron-deficiency anaemia and its nutritional causes.\n\nHigh-yield point: Iron deficiency is the commonest cause of anaemia in children worldwide, particularly during periods of rapid growth and when dietary iron is inadequate.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"CH07-Q081 — Q81: Commonest cause of anaemia in childhood","aoSource":"Question-bank MD page 104 · Printed page 185 · Answer-key PDF page 105 · Original question Q81","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Haematology_Accepted.md","_genhy":true},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-miscellaneous-topics-accepted-Q118","spec":"eb-haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Paediatric oncology","q":"Themostcommonchildhoodmalignanciesinclude","o":["Leukaemia","Retinoblastoma","Wilm'stumor","Pheochromocytoma"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Leukaemia is the most common childhood malignancy overall. Retinoblastoma and Wilms tumour are important childhood solid tumours, but pheochromocytoma is rare in childhood. The intended “except” answer is pheochromocytoma.","more":"Leukaemia is the most common childhood malignancy overall. Retinoblastoma and Wilms tumour are important childhood solid tumours, but pheochromocytoma is rare in childhood. The intended “except” answer is pheochromocytoma. **Why the others are wrong:** (a) Leukaemia: the most common childhood malignancy overall. (b) Retinoblastoma: a recognised childhood tumour. (c) Wilms tumour: a common paediatric renal tumour. (d) Pheochromocytoma: comparatively rare; correct exception.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Pediatric dermatology/allergy/hepatology/tumors/child abuse module: childhood tumours; Haematology 2024: leukaemia. **High-yield point / Review Notes:** If asked for the most common solid tumour, the answer differs from the most common malignancy overall.\n\nHigh-yield point: Leukaemia is the most common childhood malignancy overall.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q118 — Paediatric oncology","aoSource":"Question-bank MD p.162 · printed p.312 · answer-key PDF p.169 · original Q118","aoSystem":"أمراض الدم","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Miscellaneous_Topics_Accepted.md","_genhy":true}]);

